This is the most important addition for the 98% target.
Validate:
required schema fields;
allowed enum values;
data types;
string length;
array sizes;
units;
dates and times;
numeric bounds;
prohibited extra fields.
For every non-null high-risk field:
1. Require evidence text.
2. Check that the evidence occurs in the transcript.
3. Check semantic alignment between value and evidence.
4. Reject unsupported values.
Example:
Medication: amoxicillin
Transcript evidence: "start amoxicillin 500 milligrams three times daily"
→ PASS
Medication: amoxicillin
Transcript evidence: none
→ REJECT / REVIEW REQUIRED
Explicitly distinguish:
has diabetes;
denies diabetes;
history of diabetes;
rule out diabetes;
8. Clinical Validation Layer
8.1 Structural validation
8.2 Evidence validation
8.3 Negation validation

Introduce:
ACCEPT
REVIEW_REQUIRED
REJECT
Never force a value to achieve a benchmark score.
The following should default to physician review:
diagnosis;
medication;
dose;
frequency;
route;
allergies;
procedure names;
abnormal critical values;
contraindications;
patient identifiers;
discharge instructions.
A production implementation should treat these as safety-critical regardless of the model's
confidence score.
The reviewer UI should show, side-by-side:
LEFT: Original transcript
CENTER: Extracted structured values
RIGHT: Final document preview
Clicking a field should highlight the supporting transcript evidence.
